      "writtenOn": "2020-08-07T18:31:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "normal case kthreads will be true\n\npause case kthreads will be false\n\nsuggest we call this variable migrate_pinned_kthreads.\n\nThis is a neat idea, lighter than our implementation where we dequeue such tasks temporarily. Vincent\u0027s idea will be an issue if there are hundreds of pinned kthreads - we will end up skipping them many times. But we only have a handful of kernel threads so this should be fine.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6361,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6361,
        "endChar": 76
      },
      "revId": "230267a39ea985cd63e276cab127bc9b0faa648c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b6ef4521_283065da",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6361,
      "author": {
        "id": 1215505
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-18T05:51:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "if we have one RT pinned thread and 100 fair tasks, we end up calling pick_next_task() and put_prev_task() on that RT task 100 times. So one pinned thread is enough to cause overhead, I think.\n\nCan we try bookmarking the class (next\u0027s sched_class) here so that we can start from that class instead of for_each_classes()?\n\nwould it be too much to add an argument to pick_next_task() to skip pinned kthread? I like bookmarking since we don\u0027t have to duplicate the pinned kthread checks in all scheduling classes.",
      "parentUuid": "757100ae_220a7c15",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6361,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6361,
        "endChar": 76
      },
      "revId": "230267a39ea985cd63e276cab127bc9b0faa648c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
